#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163007} :activity/id #uuid "fa26629c-b4dd-57db-9661-3b43692ea236" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050535 :topic/id #uuid "51d67380-dd70-5f91-a192-de8ab2b56b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3022} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163291 :tutorial/content "We can easily extend the notion of conditional probability distributions for discrete random variables to continuous random variables.\n\nFor continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the **conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y = y$** is given by\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y} (x|y) =\\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_Y (y)}, \\qquad f_Y(y) \\neq 0,\n$$\n\nand the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X = x$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{Y|X} (y|x) =\\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_X (x)}, \\qquad f_X(x) \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that these definitions are analogous to those for discrete random variables.\n\nTo compute probabilities, we work directly with these conditional PDFs. For example, to calculate the **conditional probability of $X$ given $Y,$** we use\n\n$$\nP(X \\in A|Y = y) =\\int_A f_{X|Y} (x|y)\\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nIn general, the conditional distribution of $X$ given $Y$ does not equal the conditional distribution of $Y$  given $X$. That is\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y)  \\ne f_{Y|X}(y|x).\n$$\n\nFinally, if $X$  and $Y$  are independent, then\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y)=f_X(x),\\qquad f_{Y|X}(y|x)=f_Y(y).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4e66c559-ad43-5152-a04e-7aa8b01374ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5625 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cc469a65-06a8-5cb9-9eed-1c3d9f00c4c2" :step/math-academy-id 34590 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162112 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161678 :question/id #uuid "29e739b1-8745-517f-9011-d1761364c4c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10099"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "444ad045-3c7b-5668-bc3a-e3cb49a5b6f2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049553}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519607}] :knowledge-point/title "Working With the Definition of a Conditional PDF"} :step/id #uuid "5d645e06-ba3d-5245-bff3-396e4a4a04e4" :step/math-academy-id 38091 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162113 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161679 :question/id #uuid "3439786b-905b-5642-89d1-61b0345f79e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10100"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184d9e09-3335-5617-8989-e5ab08ca04e7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519584}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability"} :step/id #uuid "f08e3c7b-ff47-5108-9d4d-3471aa625605" :step/math-academy-id 38092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162114 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161680 :question/id #uuid "f1c9e136-57b3-59ae-90e0-b28921be44ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-10101"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb3b4b6a-3a24-5079-bf33-ed1c5576ffa7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519605}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional PDF"} :step/id #uuid "ccc00629-902d-501f-86c2-7072c1aad6a6" :step/math-academy-id 38093 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163079}}] :activity/title "Conditional Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162176}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413418} :activity/id #uuid "8d71dbb8-3f6b-51a3-b4b2-a5b2c8b23a9b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050535 :topic/id #uuid "51d67380-dd70-5f91-a192-de8ab2b56b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3022} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0232ff5b-0d05-5778-88a0-ff0af66fc111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413397} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7ebaa8-7f99-5427-8a2f-237d9f6e4410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413398} {:answer/id #uuid "291ffaa1-af34-572c-8208-632d599387b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413399} {:answer/id #uuid "2bab8694-81fe-5f61-ab63-c2f3224466f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413401} {:answer/id #uuid "cb999cce-827d-57cd-aed2-f09c922819e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413398} :answer-field/id #uuid "76914fbd-7e46-5e1e-a2ea-82b62a7c5f3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6fd86b7a-aa64-5ea3-a90b-7af5da2353f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143574" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}8xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<\\frac{1}{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{2}{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}8xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8y{\\int }_{0}^{y}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8y{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{y} \\\\  & =4y({y}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)={\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8xy}{4{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<\\frac{1}{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{2}{3}) & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{2}{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{2x}{{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{[{x}^{2}]}_{1/4}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{3}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9476e3d9-7d05-54ea-ba2e-44132f1512b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413417}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413417 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4b973f-cb9d-5e7e-8beb-4c556f2b875c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413400} {:answer/id #uuid "6a95ee3c-3035-5909-a68e-b2eac9893340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5y(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413402} {:answer/id #uuid "726964d4-b990-596e-9009-e83d6d208a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2x+3y)(y+1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413403} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e4876d-bca4-5991-b345-bf1e1f8f35e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(y+1)}{5(2x+3y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413406} {:answer/id #uuid "e77674d9-5e17-517e-8f5b-956421030e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413400} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b4844fd-48c8-5187-86c9-fcbf91d0bc32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "228badef-897c-5f4a-80d9-699c2c689782" :question/math-academy-id "q-143634" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{4xy}{5}+\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{5}+\\frac{6x{y}^{2}}{5}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8y}{5}+\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{2y}{5}-\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{6y}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6y(y+1)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6y(y+1)}{5}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5})}{(\\frac{6y(y+1)}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a4e2ce8c-ef99-5939-91ca-5637d283ed7e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413418 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76dedc47-c082-5f11-bb91-f5c53a5feef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413404} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e703b0-4bd0-5a40-af83-db0c989a5aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413405} {:answer/id #uuid "db303bfa-69cf-5cf1-86a5-b4a2624334a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413408} {:answer/id #uuid "efd7c23f-d7a0-5417-baaf-c56cd1849f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413410} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf61311-9fef-553a-a566-6bcfee2063e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413404} :answer-field/id #uuid "7133b6f3-994d-586b-8bc7-52facdb8edd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "990850d6-75f9-5e72-98e4-4c732056938a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143461" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}24xy, & \\,0<x<1,0<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1-x}24xy\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =24x{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =24x{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1-x} \\\\  & =12x(1-x)^{2}-0 \\\\  & =12x(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)={\\begin{aligned}12x(x-1)^{2}, & \\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,0<y<1-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{24xy}{12x(x-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{(x-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{(x-1)^{2}}, & \\,0<x<1,0<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}) & =P(Y<\\frac{1}{3}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{2y}{{(\\frac{1}{3}-1)}^{2}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\frac{1}{18}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cd604f93-e377-5bb5-97aa-db0cfd4f422a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413416}}] :activity/title "Conditional Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413396}]}
